    <script>
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
    const content = document.querySelector('.post-content-wrapper');
    const tocContainer = document.querySelector('#toc ul');
    if (content && tocContainer) {
        const headings = content.querySelectorAll('h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6');
        const links = [];

        headings.forEach((heading, index) => {
            const id = heading.id || `heading-${index}`;
            heading.id = id;

            const listItem = document.createElement('li');
            listItem.style.marginBottom = '1.25rem';

            const link = document.createElement('a');
            link.href = `#${id}`;
            link.textContent = heading.textContent;
            link.style.transition = 'color 0.2s';

            listItem.appendChild(link);
            tocContainer.appendChild(listItem);
            links.push({
                link,
                heading
            });
        });

        function onScroll() {
            let activeIndex = -1;
            const scrollPosition = window.scrollY || window.pageYOffset;

            links.forEach(({
                heading
            }, i) => {
                const rect = heading.getBoundingClientRect();
                const offsetTop = rect.top + window.scrollY;
                if (scrollPosition >= offsetTop - 100) {
                    activeIndex = i;
                }
            });

            links.forEach(({
                link
            }, i) => {
                if (i === activeIndex) {
                    link.classList.add('active-toc-link');
                } else {
                    link.classList.remove('active-toc-link');
                }
            });
        }

        window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, {
            passive: true
        });
        onScroll();
    }
});
</script>
  </div>
  <div class="wp-block-column is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow">
    <div class="breadcrumb-block">
      <nav aria-label="breadcrumbs" class="rank-math-breadcrumb"><p><a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com">Home</a><span class="separator"> &#8211; </span><a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/blog/category/everest/">Everest</a><span class="separator"> &#8211; </span><span class="last">How Tall is Mount Everest? Official Height in Feet and Meters</span></p></nav></div>
    <div style="padding-right:0;padding-left:0" class="entry-content alignfull post-content-wrapper wp-block-post-content has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-post-content-is-layout-9cb2c61b wp-block-post-content-is-layout-constrained">
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Mount Everest is 8,848.86 metres (29,031.7 feet) above sea level. That figure was set on 8 December 2020, when Nepal and China jointly announced the result of the first survey the two countries had ever conducted and agreed on together. It replaced the 8,848 m figure that had stood since 1955, and it is the height recognised by both governments today.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The number looks simple. Arriving at it took 164 years, six official revisions, a bilateral dispute over whether snow counts as part of a mountain, and a surveyor who lost a toe getting the reading.</p>
      <figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="576" data-attachment-id="29109" data-permalink="https://www.magicalnepal.com/blog/where-is-mount-everest-located/mount-everest-views/" data-orig-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-views.webp" data-orig-size="1920,1080" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="mount-everest-views" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-medium-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-views-300x169.webp" data-large-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-views-1024x576.webp" src="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-views-1024x576.webp" alt="Mount Everest Views" class="wp-image-29109" srcset="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-views-1024x576.webp 1024w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-views-300x169.webp 300w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-views-768x432.webp 768w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-views-1536x864.webp 1536w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-views.webp 1920w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Key Takeaways</strong></p>
      <ul class="wp-block-list">
        <li> Official height: <strong>8,848.86 m / 29,031.7 ft</strong>, or 29,031 feet 8.5 inches</li>
        <li>Confirmed by the Nepal and China joint resurvey, announced December 2020</li>
        <li>This is the <strong>snow height</strong>. The rock beneath the summit snowcap sits at 8,844.43 m</li>
        <li> Everest is the highest point above sea level, but not the tallest mountain measured base to summit</li>
        <li>The peak is still rising by roughly 2 mm a year, faster than tectonics alone explains</li>
        <li> Everest Base Camp sits at 5,364 m, and Kala Patthar at 5,545 m, around 63 percent of the summit altitude</li>
        <li>Air at the summit holds about a third of the oxygen available at sea level</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Everest sits at 27°59′18″N, 86°55′31″E, in the Mahalangur Himal sub-range of the Himalaya, roughly 160 kilometres east-northeast of Kathmandu. The China-Nepal border runs directly across the summit point, so the mountain belongs to two countries at once, falling in Solukhumbu District of Koshi Province on the Nepali side and Tingri County, Tibet on the Chinese side.</p>
      <h2 id="how-tall-is-mount-everest-in-feet-and-metres" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>How Tall Is Mount Everest in Feet and Metres?</strong></h2>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The full record of who has stood on top, and who did not come back, is in <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/mount-everest-summits-deaths-statistics/">the summit and fatality list from 1953 onward</a>.</p>
      <figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="768" data-attachment-id="29191" data-permalink="https://www.magicalnepal.com/blog/rainbow-valley-everest/mount-everest-2/" data-orig-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-1-1536x1152.jpg" data-orig-size="2560,1920" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="mount everest" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-medium-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-1-300x225.jpg" data-large-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-1-1024x768.jpg" src="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-1-1024x768.jpg.webp" alt="mount everest" class="wp-image-29191" srcset="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-1-1024x768.jpg.webp 1024w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-1-300x225.jpg.webp 300w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-1-768x576.jpg.webp 768w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-1-1536x1152.jpg.webp 1536w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/mount-everest-1-2048x1536.jpg.webp 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The official Mount Everest height in feet is <strong>29,031.7 ft</strong>, which equals 8,848.86 meters. Rounded off, that is about 8.85 kilometers or 5.5 miles of vertical distance.</p>
      <figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><tbody><tr><td><strong>Measurement</strong></td><td><strong>Metric</strong></td><td><strong>Imperial</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Official height (snow)</td><td>8,848.86 m</td><td>29,031.7 ft</td></tr><tr><td>Rock height</td><td>8,844.43 m</td><td>29,017 ft</td></tr><tr><td>Previous official figure (1955)</td><td>8,848 m</td><td>29,028 ft</td></tr><tr><td>Distance expressed as km/miles</td><td>8.85 km</td><td>5.50 mi</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Commercial aircraft cruise between 10,000 and 12,000 meters. The summit reaches into the lower edge of that band, which is why summit day gets described in aviation terms more often than hiking ones.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The mountain straddles the border between Nepal&#8217;s Solukhumbu district and the Tibet Autonomous Region, roughly 160 kilometers east of Kathmandu.</p>
      <h2 id="why-is-measuring-mount-everest-so-difficult" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Why Is Measuring Mount Everest So Difficult?</strong></h2>
      <figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="654" data-attachment-id="29228" data-permalink="https://www.magicalnepal.com/blog/how-tall-is-mount-everest/measuring-mount-everest/" data-orig-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/measuring-mount-everest.jpg" data-orig-size="1278,816" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="measuring mount everest" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-medium-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/measuring-mount-everest-300x192.jpg" data-large-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/measuring-mount-everest-1024x654.jpg" src="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/measuring-mount-everest-1024x654.jpg.webp" alt="measuring mount everest" class="wp-image-29228" srcset="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/measuring-mount-everest-1024x654.jpg.webp 1024w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/measuring-mount-everest-300x192.jpg.webp 300w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/measuring-mount-everest-768x490.jpg.webp 768w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/measuring-mount-everest.jpg.webp 1278w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Four independent problems have to be solved before a single number can be published, and each one has produced disagreement at some point in the last two centuries.</p>
      <h3 id="sea-level-has-to-be-extrapolated-inland" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Sea Level Has to Be Extrapolated Inland</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Height above sea level is measured against the geoid, a modelled surface representing where the oceans would settle if they extended through the continents. Establishing that surface under the Himalaya means carrying a levelling network hundreds of kilometres north from the plains, with every station introducing a small error.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">This is also where Nepal and China had historically diverged. Nepal carried its reference north from the southern plains, while China worked from the Yellow Sea, and two different starting points produced two different answers for the same summit.</p>
      <h3 id="the-himalaya-distorts-its-own-measurement" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>The Himalaya Distorts Its Own Measurement</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The mass of the range exerts enough gravitational pull to deflect a plumb line and warp the local geoid, meaning the reference surface beneath Everest is not where a global model would place it. Correcting for this requires a dedicated gravity survey of the region, which is precisely why Nepal&#8217;s 2019 fieldwork included gravimetric readings alongside satellite positioning.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Every survey conducted before gravity modelling became practical carried this distortion unresolved. That is a large part of why the 1856 and 1955 figures differed by eight metres despite both being carefully executed.</p>
      <h3 id="atmospheric-refraction-bends-the-sightline" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Atmospheric Refraction Bends the Sightline</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Measuring the summit angle from a station 100 kilometres away means looking through a hundred kilometres of air of varying density, which bends light and shifts the apparent position of the peak. Surveyors correct for this mathematically, but the correction depends on temperature and pressure conditions that change hour by hour.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Readings are therefore taken at sunrise, when the atmosphere is most stable and stratified. Nepal&#8217;s ground teams in 2019 held position at eight separate sites with a clear line to the summit specifically to catch that window.</p>
      <h3 id="the-summit-itself-is-not-solid" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>The Summit Itself Is Not Solid</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Everest&#8217;s highest point is a cap of compacted snow and ice sitting on rock, and the depth of that cap varies with season, snowfall and wind scouring. Measuring the rock beneath requires ground-penetrating radar operated at 8,848 m, which no one managed until 2019.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">This is the problem that produced two official heights rather than one.</p>
      <div class="mn-cta"><div class="mn-cta__txt"><p class="mn-cta__title">Planning a trek in Nepal?</p><p class="mn-cta__sub">Connect with an expert &mdash; real trek leaders in Kathmandu, free advice, no pressure.</p></div><a class="mn-cta__btn" href="https://wa.me/9779851402018?text=Hi%21%20I%27m%20planning%20a%20trek%20in%20Nepal%20and%20would%20love%20some%20expert%20advice.%20%28Reading%3A%20How%20Tall%20is%20Mount%20Everest%3F%20Official%20Height%20in%20Feet%20and%20Meters%29" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Plan now &rarr;</a></div><h2 id="why-does-mount-everest-have-two-official-heights" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Why Does Mount Everest Have Two Official Heights?</strong></h2>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Everest carries two official figures because Nepal and China spent decades measuring two different things. Nepal measured the top of the permanent snow and ice cap, while China measured the rock beneath it.</p>
      <figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><tbody><tr><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Snow height</td><td>Rock height</td></tr><tr><td>Current figure</td><td>8,848.86 m (29,031.7 ft)</td><td>8,844.43 m (29,017 ft)</td></tr><tr><td>What it measures</td><td>Top of the permanent snowcap</td><td>Bedrock summit beneath the snow</td></tr><tr><td>Established by</td><td>Nepal and China joint survey, 2020</td><td>Chinese Academy of Sciences, 2005</td></tr><tr><td>Difference</td><td>Baseline for all records</td><td>About 3.5 m of snow and ice lower</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>
      <h3 id="1-where-the-two-figures-came-from" class="wp-block-heading">1. <strong>Where the Two Figures Came From</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Survey of India revised Everest from 8,840 m to 8,848 m in 1955, and a Chinese survey reconfirmed a near-identical 8,848.13 m in 1975. Both were snow-height measurements, so for two decades there was no real disagreement.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The split opened in 2005, when China published 8,844.43 m, more than four metres below the figure Nepal recognised. The Chinese team had measured the rock base rather than the snow and ice above it, arriving at 8,844.43 m of rock plus roughly 3.5 m of snow, which totals the same 8,848 m Nepal was already using.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">In other words, the two countries had never disagreed about how tall the mountain was. They disagreed about where a mountain ends.</p>
      <h3 id="2-why-the-2010-agreement-recognised-both" class="wp-block-heading">2. <strong>Why the 2010 Agreement Recognised Both</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Rather than force a single figure, talks in Kathmandu in April 2010 produced a dual-recognition arrangement. Both governments agreed to recognise two separate heights, one based on rock and one on snow, with 8,848 m as the official figure and 8,844 m acknowledged as the rock height.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">That compromise was held for five years. The 2015 Gorkha earthquake reopened the question after geologists observed that sections of the Himalaya, including the ground around Everest, appeared to have dropped, and Nepal launched its own measurement project in 2017 at a cost of $2.5 million.</p>
      <h3 id="3-which-figure-should-be-used" class="wp-block-heading">3. <strong>Which Figure Should Be Used?</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Snow height, in almost every context. It is the convention applied to every other peak on Earth, since mountains are measured to their actual highest point rather than their bedrock, and it is the figure both governments announced jointly in December 2020.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Rock height matters in two narrow cases: geological research tracking uplift and erosion, and comparisons where seasonal snow accumulation would distort the result. The 2020 joint survey never produced an agreed rock height, because Nepali and Chinese data on that measurement did not match and the two sides dropped that part of the collaboration.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The 2005 figure of 8,844.43 m therefore remains the only rock height in circulation, and it is fifteen years older than the snow figure beside it.</p>
      <h2 id="who-first-measured-everest-and-why-is-it-named-everest" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Who First Measured Everest, and Why Is It Named Everest?</strong></h2>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Radhanath Sikdar, a Bengali mathematician employed as a chief computer on the Great Trigonometrical Survey of India, completed the calculations in 1852 that identified a peak catalogued only as Peak XV as the highest in the world. The Surveyor General, Andrew Waugh, spent four years verifying the work before publishing 29,002 feet in 1856.</p>
      <figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="699" data-attachment-id="29237" data-permalink="https://www.magicalnepal.com/blog/how-tall-is-mount-everest/radhanath-sikdar/" data-orig-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/radhanath-sikdar.jpg" data-orig-size="1242,848" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="radhanath sikdar" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-medium-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/radhanath-sikdar-300x205.jpg" data-large-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/radhanath-sikdar-1024x699.jpg" src="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/radhanath-sikdar-1024x699.jpg.webp" alt="radhanath sikdar" class="wp-image-29237" srcset="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/radhanath-sikdar-1024x699.jpg.webp 1024w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/radhanath-sikdar-300x205.jpg.webp 300w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/radhanath-sikdar-768x524.jpg.webp 768w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/radhanath-sikdar.jpg.webp 1242w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The measurement was taken from six observation stations on the plains of what is now northern India and Nepal&#8217;s Terai, some more than 170 kilometres from the summit. Nepal was closed to foreign surveyors at the time, so no one involved ever approached the mountain.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The naming followed in 1865, when the Royal Geographical Society adopted Waugh&#8217;s proposal to honour his predecessor George Everest. Everest himself objected, on the grounds that the name could not be written in Hindi or pronounced by local people, and he never saw the mountain that carries his name. The peak already had names: Sagarmatha in Nepali and Chomolungma in Tibetan.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The 29,002-foot figure is also the origin of a long-running story. Surveyors reportedly arrived at exactly 29,000 feet and added two feet so the result would not be mistaken for a rounded estimate.</p>
      <h2 id="how-has-everest-s-official-height-changed-since-1856" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>How Has Everest&#8217;s Official Height Changed Since 1856?</strong></h2>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Everest has carried at least six different official heights in under two centuries. The changes reflect improving instruments rather than a mountain that grew several metres.</p>
      <figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><tbody><tr><td>Year</td><td>Survey</td><td>Figure</td><td>Method</td></tr><tr><td>1856</td><td>Great Trigonometrical Survey of India</td><td>8,840 m (29,002 ft)</td><td>Theodolite triangulation from the plains, over 100 miles away</td></tr><tr><td>1955</td><td>Survey of India</td><td>8,848 m (29,028 ft)</td><td>Triangulation from closer stations inside Nepal</td></tr><tr><td>1975</td><td>Chinese survey</td><td>8,848.13 m</td><td>Summit-based measurement, snow height</td></tr><tr><td>1999</td><td>American Millennium Expedition</td><td>8,850 m (29,035 ft)</td><td>First GPS receiver placed on the summit</td></tr><tr><td>2005</td><td>Chinese Academy of Sciences</td><td>8,844.43 m</td><td>Ground-penetrating radar to find the rock surface</td></tr><tr><td>2010</td><td>Nepal and China bilateral agreement</td><td>8,848 m snow / 8,844 m rock</td><td>Dual recognition, no new survey</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>Nepal and China joint resurvey</td><td>8,848.86 m (29,031.7 ft)</td><td>GNSS, gravimetry, shared geoid model</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The 1999 figure deserves a note, because it circulated widely for two decades. The American Millennium Expedition placed the first GPS unit on the summit and published 8,850 m, which National Geographic adopted and a number of American expedition operators promoted, since it added a couple of metres to what their clients had climbed.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">What is striking across the whole sequence is how little the total moved. The 2020 figure sits 8.86 m above an 1856 measurement made with theodolites, human arithmetic and no access to the mountain.</p>
      <h2 id="how-did-nepal-and-china-settle-the-everest-elevation-in-2020" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>How Did Nepal and China Settle the Everest Elevation in 2020?</strong></h2>
      <h3 id="why-two-countries-had-to-agree-at-all" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Why Two Countries Had to Agree at All</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The China-Nepal border runs directly across the summit point, so neither country can claim sole authority over the official figure. Nepal had been working toward its own measurement since 2011 and fully intended to publish independently, having already turned down an Indian offer to run the survey jointly.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">That changed for diplomatic rather than technical reasons. Four months after Nepal&#8217;s field surveyors finished their work, Xi Jinping arrived in Kathmandu on 12 October 2019 for a state visit, and the two countries announced during it that they would cooperate on repairing the peak and release the findings together.</p>
      <h3 id="what-nepal-s-survey-team-actually-did" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>What Nepal&#8217;s Survey Team Actually Did</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Khim Lal Gautam and Rabin Karki of Nepal&#8217;s Survey Department, with guide Tshiring Jangbu Sherpa, reached the summit on 22 May 2019 carrying more than 40 kilograms of equipment, including a GNSS receiver and ground-penetrating radar. The ascent was timed to reach the top around 3 a.m., in darkness and the coldest part of the night, so the team could work on an empty summit during one of the busiest seasons on record.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Gautam and Karki spent two hours working at the highest point on Earth. Gautam, who had already summited Everest in 2011, developed frostbite during the effort and later lost a toe to amputation. It was the first time any surveyor had collected data directly from the summit rather than measuring it from a distance.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The summit work was one component of four. Nepal ran precise levelling from the southern plains to establish control points, trigonometric levelling from those points, a gravity survey to define the local geoid, and GNSS positioning to fix both the benchmarks and the summit itself.</p>
      <h2 id="how-does-everest-s-height-compare-to-other-mountains-and-landmarks" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>How Does Everest&#8217;s Height Compare to Other Mountains and Landmarks?</strong></h2><section class="mbfse-hc" aria-label="Compare Everest's height" data-hc="{&quot;k2&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;K2&quot;,&quot;m&quot;:&quot;8,611&quot;,&quot;ft&quot;:&quot;28,251&quot;,&quot;pct&quot;:97,&quot;where&quot;:&quot;Karakoram, Pakistan and China&quot;,&quot;sx&quot;:0.5,&quot;sy&quot;:0.07,&quot;img&quot;:&quot;https:\/\/www.magicalnepal.com\/wp-content\/themes\/magical-nepal\/assets\/img\/height-compare\/k2.webp&quot;},&quot;denali&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Denali&quot;,&quot;m&quot;:&quot;6,190&quot;,&quot;ft&quot;:&quot;20,310&quot;,&quot;pct&quot;:70,&quot;where&quot;:&quot;Alaska, USA&quot;,&quot;sx&quot;:0.56,&quot;sy&quot;:0.1,&quot;img&quot;:&quot;https:\/\/www.magicalnepal.com\/wp-content\/themes\/magical-nepal\/assets\/img\/height-compare\/denali.webp&quot;},&quot;kilimanjaro&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Kilimanjaro&quot;,&quot;m&quot;:&quot;5,895&quot;,&quot;ft&quot;:&quot;19,341&quot;,&quot;pct&quot;:67,&quot;where&quot;:&quot;Tanzania&quot;,&quot;sx&quot;:0.5,&quot;sy&quot;:0.06,&quot;img&quot;:&quot;https:\/\/www.magicalnepal.com\/wp-content\/themes\/magical-nepal\/assets\/img\/height-compare\/kilimanjaro.webp&quot;},&quot;kalapatthar&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Kala Patthar viewpoint&quot;,&quot;m&quot;:&quot;5,545&quot;,&quot;ft&quot;:&quot;18,192&quot;,&quot;pct&quot;:63,&quot;where&quot;:&quot;Khumbu, Nepal&quot;,&quot;sx&quot;:0.4,&quot;sy&quot;:0.5,&quot;img&quot;:&quot;https:\/\/www.magicalnepal.com\/wp-content\/themes\/magical-nepal\/assets\/img\/height-compare\/kalapatthar.webp&quot;},&quot;montblanc&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Mont Blanc&quot;,&quot;m&quot;:&quot;4,808&quot;,&quot;ft&quot;:&quot;15,774&quot;,&quot;pct&quot;:54,&quot;where&quot;:&quot;Alps, France and Italy&quot;,&quot;sx&quot;:0.4,&quot;sy&quot;:0.18,&quot;img&quot;:&quot;https:\/\/www.magicalnepal.com\/wp-content\/themes\/magical-nepal\/assets\/img\/height-compare\/montblanc.webp&quot;},&quot;burjkhalifa&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Burj Khalifa&quot;,&quot;m&quot;:&quot;828&quot;,&quot;ft&quot;:&quot;2,717&quot;,&quot;pct&quot;:9,&quot;where&quot;:&quot;Dubai, UAE&quot;,&quot;sx&quot;:0.5,&quot;sy&quot;:0.05,&quot;img&quot;:&quot;https:\/\/www.magicalnepal.com\/wp-content\/themes\/magical-nepal\/assets\/img\/height-compare\/burjkhalifa.webp&quot;}}">
