      </section>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Kala Patthar figure is the one that matters for visitors, since <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/blog/kala-patthar/">that rocky spur above Gorak Shep</a> is the highest point in the Khumbu reachable without a climbing permit.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">K2 sits only 238 m lower yet has a fatality rate several times higher, a gap explained by technical difficulty, remoteness and weather rather than altitude.</p>
      <h3 id="everest-is-an-outlier-even-among-the-8-000-metre-peaks" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Everest Is an Outlier Even Among the 8,000-Metre Peaks</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Everest rises about 250 metres above the next tallest peak in the Himalaya, which is a far larger gap than separates the peaks immediately below it. K2, Kangchenjunga and Lhotse all sit within roughly 100 metres of one another, making Everest&#8217;s margin anomalous rather than expected.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">That anomaly is not just trivia. It is the observation that prompted the research explaining why Everest is still growing faster than the mountains around it.</p>
      <h2 id="is-everest-the-tallest-mountain-on-earth" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Is Everest the Tallest Mountain on Earth?</strong></h2>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Everest is the highest point above sea level, which is a different claim from being the tallest mountain. Two other peaks beat it on alternative measures.</p>
      <ul class="wp-block-list">
        <li><strong>Mauna Kea, Hawaii</strong> rises 10,210 m from its base on the Pacific seafloor, of which only 4,207 m sits above water. Measured base to summit, it is the tallest mountain on the planet.</li>
        <li><strong>Chimborazo, Ecuador</strong> sits near the equatorial bulge, so its summit is roughly 2,100 m further from the centre of the Earth than Everest&#8217;s, despite reaching only 6,263 m above sea level.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Everest also has a modest base-to-summit rise by mountaineering standards, because the Tibetan Plateau it sits on is already above 4,000 m. Measured from Base Camp, the climb is around 3,500 m, less than the vertical relief on Denali or Nanga Parbat.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">What Everest holds uniquely is altitude, which is the measure that governs climbing, aviation and human physiology.</p>
      <h2 id="is-mount-everest-still-getting-taller" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Is Mount Everest Still Getting Taller?</strong></h2>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yes, and faster than plate tectonics alone accounts for. The Indian plate continues to push beneath the Eurasian plate, lifting the range while moving it northeast at roughly 45 mm per year, but GPS measurements show Everest and its neighbours rising at about 2 mm annually, above the long-term trend.</p>
      <h3 id="the-river-that-is-lifting-everest" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>The River That Is Lifting Everest</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">A 2024 paper in Nature Geoscience attributes part of that recent uptick to isostatic rebound following a river capture event. Researchers modelling erosion rates in the Arun and Kosi systems concluded that around 89,000 years ago the Arun merged into the Kosi network, a process called drainage piracy.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The redirected water gave the Kosi far greater erosive power, and the gorge it has been carving roughly 75 kilometres from Everest has removed enough rock for the crust to spring upward in compensation, adding between 15 and 50 metres to the peak over that period. The same rebound is lifting Lhotse and Makalu, the fourth and fifth highest mountains on Earth.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The authors present this as the best current explanation for the gap between Everest&#8217;s height and what tectonics predicts, built on landscape modelling rather than direct observation, and frame it as a compelling hypothesis rather than settled fact.</p>
      <h3 id="what-works-against-the-growth" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>What Works Against the Growth</strong></h3>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Erosion, snowfall variation and seismic events all pull in the other direction. Following the April 2015 Gorkha earthquake, several studies suggested the summit may have dropped by around 2.5 cm, which was one of the reasons Nepal commissioned the resurvey in the first place.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">At 2 mm a year, Everest gains about 2 cm per decade, well inside the margin of error of most surveys. The next revision to the official figure will come from better instruments, not from a taller mountain.</p>
      <h2 id="what-does-8-848-86-meters-actually-feel-like" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>What does 8,848.86 Meters Actually Feel Like?</strong></h2>
      <figure class="wp-block-image size-large is-resized"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="655" data-attachment-id="29229" data-permalink="https://www.magicalnepal.com/blog/how-tall-is-mount-everest/mount-everest-height/" data-orig-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/mount-everest-height.jpg" data-orig-size="1275,816" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;}" data-image-title="mount everest height" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-medium-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/mount-everest-height-300x192.jpg" data-large-file="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/mount-everest-height-1024x655.jpg" src="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/mount-everest-height-1024x655.jpg.webp" alt="mount everest height" class="wp-image-29229" style="aspect-ratio:1.5633752071673912;width:801px;height:auto" srcset="https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/mount-everest-height-1024x655.jpg.webp 1024w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/mount-everest-height-300x192.jpg.webp 300w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/mount-everest-height-768x492.jpg.webp 768w, https://www.magicalnepal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/mount-everest-height.jpg.webp 1275w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Numbers on a page do not carry the physiology. Air pressure at the summit is about one-third of sea level, meaning each breath delivers roughly a third of the oxygen a body expects.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Above 8,000 meters, the human body cannot acclimatize at all. It only deteriorates. Climbers call that band the death zone, and the stretch of the north-side descent known as <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/blog/rainbow-valley-everest/">Rainbow Valley</a> takes its name from the jackets of those who never came down.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Oxygen saturation starts dropping noticeably for most trekkers around 4,400 m. Headaches, broken sleep and loss of appetite are the <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/travel-guide/everest/everest-base-camp-trek-altitude-sickness/">early warning signs worth taking seriously</a> rather than walking through.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">One detail surprises almost everyone: Everest is not visible from Base Camp. The Khumbu ridgeline hides it completely.</p>
      <h2 id="where-can-you-actually-see-the-summit-from" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Where Can You Actually See the Summit From?</strong></h2>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The clearest view on the standard route comes from Kala Patthar, which is why groups leave Gorak Shep at 4 am to be on that ridge at sunrise. Base Camp itself is a glacier moraine with prayer flags on it, and the only Everest feature visible from there is the <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/blog/khumbu-icefall/">Khumbu Icefall</a> grinding down from the Western Cwm.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Several routes deliver the summit without going all the way to Base Camp:</p>
      <ul class="wp-block-list">
        <li>The <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/trip/gokyo-lake-trek/">Gokyo Lake trek</a> climbs Gokyo Ri for a wider panorama that takes in Everest, Lhotse, Makalu and Cho Oyu at once.</li>
        <li>Trekkers with three weeks and strong legs cross Kongma La, Cho La and Renjo La on the <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/trip/everest-three-high-pass-trek/">Everest Three High Pass trek</a>, which strings all the major viewpoints together.</li>
        <li>Nine days is enough for the <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/trip/tengboche-everest-view-trek/">Tengboche Everest View trek</a>, which tops out below 4,000 m and suits families or anyone wary of high altitude.</li>
        <li>Time-poor visitors can reach the amphitheatre and return the same morning on an <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/trip/everest-base-camp-helicopter-tour/">Everest Base Camp helicopter tour</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="what-does-it-cost-to-reach-everest-s-height" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>What does it cost to Reach Everest&#8217;s Height?</strong></h2>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Two completely different price brackets, depending on how high the objective sits.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Trekking to Base Camp</strong> needs a <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/blog/sagarmatha-national-park/">Sagarmatha National Park</a> entry ticket plus a Khumbu Pasang Lhamu Rural Municipality card, and both are <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/travel-guide/everest/everest-base-camp-trek-permit/">issued at fixed government rates</a> that change most years. The standard <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/trip/everest-base-camp-trek/">Everest Base Camp trek</a> runs 14 days from Kathmandu and costs around USD 1500 &#8211; USD 2500 based on how you travel.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Climbing to the summit</strong> is a different scale entirely. Nepal raised the spring climbing royalty from USD 11,000 to USD 15,000 per foreign climber, effective 1 September 2025, the first increase since 2015. Autumn permits cost USD 7,500, and winter or monsoon permits USD 3,750.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">That royalty is only the government&#8217;s share. Full expedition packages on the south side average around USD 55,000 to 60,000, permit validity dropped from 75 days to 55 in the same rule change, and one guide is now mandatory for every two climbers on any peak above 8,000 meters.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Almost nobody arrives on Everest as a first Himalayan objective. The usual progression starts with a 6,000 m trekking peak, and <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/trip/island-peak-climbing/">Island Peak</a> at 6,189 m is the most common choice because the summit ridge and glacier crossing teach fixed-rope and crampon technique under real conditions.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Expedition operators generally want to see one of those climbs on a CV before selling an Everest place. <a href="https://www.magicalnepal.com/trip/mera-peak-climbing/">Mera Peak</a> at 6,476 m is the alternative for climbers who want altitude without technical ice.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">None of the climbing rules apply to trekkers. Confusion between the two permit systems is the single most common question that reaches the Magical Nepal inbox each spring.</p>
      <h2 id="conclusion" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Conclusion</strong></h2>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">Mount Everest stands 8,848.86 meters, or 29,031.7 feet, above sea level. That figure has held since 8 December 2020, when Nepal and China accepted a single number for the first time in the mountain&#8217;s surveyed history, and it counts the snow cap the way climbers experience it rather than the bedrock underneath.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">The number is precise, but it is not permanent. The summit rises about 2 millimeters a year, and the next survey will almost certainly report something slightly different.</p>
      <p class="wp-block-paragraph">What does not change is the scale. Base Camp reaches 61 percent of that height and already sits above the altitude where the body starts to struggle. The remaining 39 percent is where Everest stops being a destination and becomes a mountaineering problem.</p>
      <h2 id="frequently-asked-questions-1" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Frequently Asked Questions</strong></h2>
      <div id="rank-math-faq" class="mbfse-faq">
        <div class="rank-math-list ">
          <div id="faq-question-1789646101969" class="mbfse-faq__item">
            <h3 class="mbfse-faq__question "><strong>How Tall Is Mount Everest in Feet Exactly?</strong></h3>
            <div class="mbfse-faq__answer ">
              <p>29,031.7 feet, or 29,031 feet and 8.5 inches.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div id="faq-question-1789646113164" class="mbfse-faq__item">
            <h3 class="mbfse-faq__question "><strong>How Much Higher Is Everest Than K2?</strong></h3>
            <div class="mbfse-faq__answer ">
              <p>238 metres, or 781 feet.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div id="faq-question-1789646122651" class="mbfse-faq__item">
            <h3 class="mbfse-faq__question "><strong>Who First Calculated That Everest Was the World&#8217;s Highest Mountain?</strong></h3>
            <div class="mbfse-faq__answer ">
              <p>Radhanath Sikdar, a Bengali mathematician working for the Great Trigonometrical Survey of India, completed the computation in 1852. The Surveyor General published the result in 1856, identifying the peak then catalogued as Peak XV.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div id="faq-question-1789646133307" class="mbfse-faq__item">
            <h3 class="mbfse-faq__question "><strong>Can Everest&#8217;s Summit Be Seen From Kathmandu?</strong></h3>
            <div class="mbfse-faq__answer ">
              <p>No. The ranges visible from Kathmandu Valley viewpoints are Langtang and Ganesh Himal. Everest sits too far east and is screened by closer peaks, which is why scenic mountain flights depart Kathmandu specifically to bring passengers within sight of it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div id="faq-question-1789646142153" class="mbfse-faq__item">
            <h3 class="mbfse-faq__question "><strong>Why Is Everest Measured in Meters With Decimals but Feet Without?</strong></h3>
            <div class="mbfse-faq__answer ">
              <p>The survey was conducted metrically, so 8,848.86 m is the measured value and every foot figure is a conversion. Quoting 29,031.7 ft implies a precision of a tenth of a foot that the original measurement does not claim, which is why most publications round to the nearest foot.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div id="faq-question-1789646152409" class="mbfse-faq__item">
            <h3 class="mbfse-faq__question "><strong>How Long Does It Take to Walk to the Base of Everest?</strong></h3>
            <div class="mbfse-faq__answer ">
              <p>Eight days from Lukla to Everest Base Camp on the standard itinerary, including two acclimatization days, with four to five days for the descent, where Everest stops being a destination and becomes a mountaineering problem.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
